ane up 12%, mostly substations and transformers. Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. The documents don't give the total equity gap. The only sizing is Pellham's planned C$2.4B raise.\n- **Issuance window:** The window is poor.\n  - Pellham withdrew that C$2.4B offering because investor demand fell short.\n  - Two other utility share offerings were cut back in size.\n  - Bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp, yet Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably.\n  - Debt is open and equity is not. Utilities will have to lean on debt, which limits how far spreads can widen before it gets costly.\n- **Valuations and dilution:** I found no data on valuation multiples, share prices or share counts, so I can't quantify dilution. Demand fell short even at the planned pricing, so any deal that does get done would likely need a bigger discount, and dilution would be heavier. That is an inference, not something the documents state.\n- **Consequence:** Pellham will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. Capex plans are being scaled back rather than funded through equity. This is negative for grid-equipment demand from Pellham.\n\n**What would change this:**\n- A successful follow-on offering.\n- Tighter bond spreads.\n- Pellham reinstating its deferred programmes.\n\nThe debt side of this question is covered by n3 and n5.","ttft_ms":1376,"type":"result","duration_ms":24742,"uuid":"d23fc378-25dd-4221-a10c-3162ba1f9482","ttft_stream_ms":875,"time_to_request_ms":67,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":3,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":5,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":8,"message_normalization":